design-code-rules.requirement-text:
Designing green infrastructure to perform multiple functions will deliver schemes that make efficient use of land and are cost effective. Combining recreation, movement, drainage and enhancing biodiversity in the same space will maximise benefits and make the best use of space available.

A well-connected and multifunctional green infrastructure system should be integrated holistically into the landscape design. This should incorporate:

- public open spaces (see Chapter 5);
- movement network (see Chapter 4 and Chapter 7);
- SuDS network (see section 3.4 Sustainable Drainage);
- wildlife habitat network (see section 3.2 Biodiversity), and;
- development edges, to blend seamlessly with green infrastructure outside of the site.

(For a more comprehensive list see table 9 of the East Riding Local Plan.)

Green infrastructure should be designed to integrate existing neighbouring areas and future developments. Public open spaces and streets should include new trees and landscaping elements that complement and enhance existing features while mitigating any negative impacts caused by the new development.

Connecting green infrastructure will maximise benefits, create resilience and provide continuous corridors for wildlife and movement of people.

The green infrastructure system should connect existing and proposed natural and semi-natural spaces, including:

- open spaces;
- wooded areas;
- tree belts and hedgerows;
- wildlife habitats;
- green corridors;
- SuDS and water bodies.

Further information on integrating quality green infrastructure into development can be found in Building with Nature.

The National Urban Greening Factor should be met as follows:

- 0.3 for commercial development;
- 0.4 for residential development, and;
- 0.5 for residential greenfield development.

Guidance on using the urban greening factor is set out in Natural England’s Green Infrastructure Framework. For the urban greening factor, the evidence of calculations should be submitted with applications.

To improve biodiversity on site, a network should be created, that connects existing and proposed wildlife habitats. These should be integrated into the green and blue infrastructure network.

Throughout the whole site, including public and private spaces, development should:

- retain, protect and enhance existing natural features such as trees, hedgerows, wildflower meadows and water courses;
- protect and create wildlife habitats and corridors through whole sites, connecting to green infrastructure outside of the site boundary (see section 3.1 Green Infrastructure);
- use existing hedgerows, amending any gaps, to define boundaries and create wildlife corridors linking these to other wildlife habitats;
- include native trees and plants in landscaping schemes;
- enhance resilience by including climate-resistant trees and plants in landscaping schemes;
- use retained trees and include additional trees in car parking, communal areas inside blocks and into streets;
- integrate sustainable drainage plans with landscaping and use the SuDS solutions designed into the site to promote biodiversity;
- Consider integrating green walls and green roofs;
- Consider use of brown roofs;
- include locally appropriate wildflower and species-rich planting;
- include integrated bat and swift bricks supplemented with species specific nest boxes where appropriate. The use of generic bird boxes is discouraged.

Biodiversity Net Gain (BNG) metrics must be submitted with applications to comply with policy.

design-code-rules.guidance-text:
The SuDS should be incorporated into individual plots, streets, public open spaces and wildlife networks. The design of SuDS should also:

- take advantage of the site’s natural topography to minimise the need for mechanical measures;
- effectively manage run-off;
- enhance natural filtration and infiltration to improve water quality and;
- include provisions for water storage for irrigation to reduce the demand for additional water supply.

There are different methods for including SuDS in a development. Which method is most appropriate will depend on the type of development, scale of development and ground conditions. Where possible, SuDS should be designed to maximise biodiversity value. Table 3 sets out some possible SuDS methods.

On sites with nutrient neutrality issues SuDS may not be appropriate. A site-specific approach based on mitigation guidance should be taken.
